#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157107 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "1abed37b-99f8-5298-bc7b-f80259c78398" :question/math-academy-id "e-7446" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/1a/1a75a5242f3636efac8def7fc62458e1c1c6972ca79a1ab6fd73af47be622a7a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5),$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-4,5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ -2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157108 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "af2757f8-0392-54c8-b681-162ee13b1804" :question/math-academy-id "e-7447" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/83/8355891fbc605593a32dd100ee8c1c2e4324da0630755984158ec23f6d419ab9.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-3,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157109 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "780170af-a92e-5a87-b08d-d9d940d6906b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7448" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y+1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-1}{2}) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-8(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0303266d-f1ac-53c8-9223-9776f8286eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=-4t,\\,\\,y-3=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486599} {:answer/id #uuid "923d4e3a-5bcb-5052-8780-5da21cc38f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-2t,\\,\\,y-3=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486914} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f9e45c-7062-5f28-bc03-f34206a84654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-4t,\\,\\,y-3=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486960} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7bd60d-4e3f-5c71-ba73-a9b888732f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-4t,\\,\\,y+3=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487001} {:answer/id #uuid "db34ee1a-a069-58c3-b395-c6c0f3a56659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-t,\\,\\,y-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0f9e45c-7062-5f28-bc03-f34206a84654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-4t,\\,\\,y-3=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486960} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf911a04-5f91-5fae-b9c3-9dfaff9a03ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f7086f8-f36f-50c7-ad0c-2ef637d5cf6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106454" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(-2,3)$ and which passes through the point $(-10,-5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-10,-5),$ we can substitute $x=-10$ and $y=-5$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8=2pt \\\\ -8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(-\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{2}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=2(-2)t,\\,y-3=(-2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=-4t,\\,y-3=-2{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53b2515b-c470-5df9-b5f0-72da0d8839dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53b2515b-c470-5df9-b5f0-72da0d8839dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300393} :answer-field/id #uuid "4339e742-90db-5fe5-8713-242c87bd162a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301454} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f966e1-32ba-571c-8d2f-139d236a9efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01f966e1-32ba-571c-8d2f-139d236a9efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299939} :answer-field/id #uuid "d17ed819-e5df-5f2a-85be-61fbd0c56120" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df968d14-24c9-53a0-ac49-2683ed9a8cca" :question/math-academy-id "q-321592" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=5t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+2}{5},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25} & =y-2 \\\\ {(x+2)}^{2} & =25(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6342237f-0750-5d97-83a5-6340dfaa32e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6342237f-0750-5d97-83a5-6340dfaa32e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486811} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ad4200b-af15-5126-a1e0-17c3d7a4e5d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "900f227c-0435-5540-af63-e96714d948bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "900f227c-0435-5540-af63-e96714d948bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486911} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dc01537-89dc-53d0-b0f7-16752ad38f83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2210e304-f1be-5ab9-a627-e9a4a8410a48" :question/math-academy-id "q-323132" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-1=3t,\\,y+4={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-1}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y+4$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =y+4 \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2} & =9(y+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239c0bea-6ec4-55a9-ba55-06444b1b6005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "239c0bea-6ec4-55a9-ba55-06444b1b6005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486665} :answer-field/id #uuid "60677215-48aa-5d3e-81b4-2fe26b9d5615" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487187} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df2fba26-5f67-5068-9b55-1835d94b0f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df2fba26-5f67-5068-9b55-1835d94b0f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487079} :answer-field/id #uuid "987f26a7-d980-5f3c-aef7-b7ef1d95d1d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4ed1fe6-d320-5a32-bfb8-73846e898159" :question/math-academy-id "q-323212" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-4=3{t}^{2},\\,y+2=6t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x-4}{3},\\,t=\\frac{y+2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y+2}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x-4}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{x-4}{3} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =36\\cdot (\\frac{x-4}{3}) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =12(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e9ab5f9-e0df-5f65-a021-59f32c7c7165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=2t,\\,y-6={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486650} {:answer/id #uuid "50ed4773-3ed9-5b89-9333-b39bf928515e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1={t}^{2},\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486772} {:answer/id #uuid "54fff804-525b-5500-af11-d8004b02fdf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486779} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb30a1c-48e6-50f2-b4fa-5876780a57ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487010} {:answer/id #uuid "f03d06b8-4245-5331-81e6-9913e1da8421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-t,\\,y+6=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54fff804-525b-5500-af11-d8004b02fdf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486779} :answer-field/id #uuid "791d2dca-a053-5eb5-a884-1ab282013d73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e847f0a2-135f-5fe9-88a5-7da062fb29a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-84459" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-2)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,6)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,6),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(-\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y-(-2)=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bcdaab6-34bc-5cf0-920a-1920fb37788d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352521} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf23584-4cc5-5a62-b7de-fb2e55c64458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352909} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9ef262-bb28-5e12-ad5e-ab3f87650177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353173} {:answer/id #uuid "e3106343-e7c0-5b6b-8a50-8d6ac691410c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=\\frac{5}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353542} {:answer/id #uuid "ff638889-0eb9-5f29-bb21-b3200514aa48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=\\frac{5}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc9ef262-bb28-5e12-ad5e-ab3f87650177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353173} :answer-field/id #uuid "abaff4b2-cd37-5ce8-930c-1cb1903f7b7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9821351-2cd3-5e5a-a4b5-1b6135a99522" :question/math-academy-id "q-84462" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-1)^{2}=-5(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-1=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p(x-1) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p & =-5 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=(-\\frac{5}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y-1=2(-\\frac{5}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39529e1e-2088-505f-9e34-bbc1c3be2f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=2t,\\,y-2=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486715} {:answer/id #uuid "471851da-2777-566c-aca4-80a6129293ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=-4{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486748} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7a6075-a431-53ee-bc18-5eceb959098b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-4t,\\,y-4=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486868} {:answer/id #uuid "b8700ae7-73f0-5f02-8801-a5a0722e1c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=4t,\\,y-4=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486998} {:answer/id #uuid "e149d119-0167-5e62-a13f-d6a2d2aeb067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e7a6075-a431-53ee-bc18-5eceb959098b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-4t,\\,y-4=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486868} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc264f7f-ffc3-5bf3-a557-71a3989333ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6266b55e-c4d6-52a5-b8a7-d0b0754b47be" :question/math-academy-id "q-84464" :question/problem "![](images/34/34e9beebbecc3eff77faa9b01f4684e334ec7cbdcbd9b00e328f95f7be88c1d4.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-2,4),$ and passes through the point $(2,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,4),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=2pt,\\,y-4=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(2,2),$ we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=2$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=2(-2)t,\\,y-4=(-2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=-4t,\\,y-4=-2{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302d353b-a56f-5c24-9f51-d27538caecd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-3{t}^{2},\\,y+5=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352006} {:answer/id #uuid "579d5d7a-937a-5e4b-b254-12477d1f60d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=6t,\\,y+1=3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352340} {:answer/id #uuid "83531b39-1632-5a9b-b982-06d44c0ac31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=6{t}^{2},\\,y-1=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352715} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e7406e-1f65-58f8-b3b4-5ea6927da113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353143} {:answer/id #uuid "bd29111e-fe20-58b0-b9d5-282ba37d292e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=3t,\\,y-5=6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7e7406e-1f65-58f8-b3b4-5ea6927da113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353143} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4358c3-f15f-501b-ba43-cb878c186919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "367eead1-51e7-5f05-9cdc-3a8e5cc1c6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-84467" :question/problem "![](images/75/75df54dd30da590814e89153396ac7cf8a713fc9a6e04d14786780147ba57cfe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(5,-1),$ and passes through the point $(17,11).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(5,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(17,11),$ we can substitute $x=17$ and $y=11$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}12=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(\\frac{6}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{36}{12} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(3){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23911304-8fc2-5561-9525-01cac991662b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}=24(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351888} {:answer/id #uuid "313ce0ec-4570-5871-b5c0-e74125caa971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352024} {:answer/id #uuid "7047a78d-9858-5e6a-9ecf-c0bd156ab4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}=6(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352553} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3e7980-d4d2-5567-9f8e-474402dba20f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}=6(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353046} {:answer/id #uuid "f82a350e-4f08-57f2-a427-9a9dad7ea665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae3e7980-d4d2-5567-9f8e-474402dba20f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}=6(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353046} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3a291d-30fa-5a7d-9b82-7059959aa63b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b1571ae-cba4-599c-b064-df002d19a987" :question/math-academy-id "q-84469" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-6=3t,\\,y-3=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-6}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-6}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2(y-3)}{3} \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =9\\cdot (\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}) \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =6(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441df4d0-5131-54a1-933b-f1384c81ed4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352194} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf4916d-f01a-5c33-ab05-5d7802093fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352648} {:answer/id #uuid "a9425634-d635-528c-9bd8-3e80c442d03f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353002} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1a39da-13cd-5859-89cd-ac0c7adad417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353649} {:answer/id #uuid "f1435b12-8e14-5660-ae0a-19a587cb9590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bf4916d-f01a-5c33-ab05-5d7802093fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352648} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c550a8-9f9a-5976-b63a-664e3b368ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c9ce835-d453-5565-8c95-6082447bc008" :question/math-academy-id "q-84819" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d963d5620df91a05c8371ef09beeb84546c5f5a8cda047f2c2c3b9933f3d5c01.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(2,-2),$ and passes through the point $(4,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,2),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=2$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ 4=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=2(\\frac{1}{4})t,\\,y+2=(\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d949e01-5c39-571d-88f1-dcb4d3a1a2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=-3t,\\,y+2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351986} {:answer/id #uuid "62fef945-a3f6-5c74-a2d0-6c2060091b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=-\\frac{4}{3}t,\\,y+2=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352448} {:answer/id #uuid "b78baab2-1d69-5bc2-9a5a-0a123e9033be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353136} {:answer/id #uuid "c904addc-f17e-5d67-90e8-69943b9c6fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353316} {:answer/id #uuid "eea2e79c-ed3d-5fd7-b8bc-9ec17d32242e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{4}{3}t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b78baab2-1d69-5bc2-9a5a-0a123e9033be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353136} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef32ef9c-fd4b-5387-a414-4da52d467787" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f9a3da7-8717-5e67-b44f-7ce603eaf516" :question/math-academy-id "q-84821" :question/problem "![](images/91/91500faad34245d5b2389ae71aa39f34b10b9a75b1b75307b5aa6231b311307b.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(1,2),$ and passes through the point $(7,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,-4),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=-4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6=2pt \\\\ -6=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -6 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -6 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=2(-\\frac{3}{2})t,\\,y-2=(-\\frac{3}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003023ae-1aa2-54a4-98df-d8e7ff22066d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=-{t}^{2},\\,y+3=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351563} {:answer/id #uuid "04a61724-2b44-5a05-b9c5-ee7e18a029b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=-4{t}^{2},\\,y+3=12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351612} {:answer/id #uuid "249bff29-1e23-57f0-a40d-6219e1c53f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351902} {:answer/id #uuid "252094cb-c77d-5625-a2e6-f8d7aa811973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351906} {:answer/id #uuid "d8dbece5-e2b7-5256-a030-b0f252658ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "252094cb-c77d-5625-a2e6-f8d7aa811973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351906} :answer-field/id #uuid "8926ebe9-9279-5214-b8aa-a91752679929" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1303c715-2341-5c20-852f-40641cdc1a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-84844" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-3)^{2}=-12(x-5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(5,3).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y-3=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p(x-5) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p & =-12 \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(-3){t}^{2},\\,y-3=2(-3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eb9df3b-ad51-5fd3-a5d7-0378bb8e50f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-6{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486686} {:answer/id #uuid "50351eff-4bc5-5559-b109-6e998a84a90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486767} {:answer/id #uuid "57c02f6e-98c1-5975-8d55-a608122a6888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-3{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486786} {:answer/id #uuid "6bca42c9-1e9a-518c-a98e-83ca02ecb23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-\\frac{3}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486828} {:answer/id #uuid "746886be-acda-5c82-9239-d30a7dc5ecc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bca42c9-1e9a-518c-a98e-83ca02ecb23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-\\frac{3}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486828} :answer-field/id #uuid "a437a535-c718-5028-9485-0dd9f5d01fb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0668c7c6-b240-56da-94fb-07d6ea371e42" :question/math-academy-id "q-84845" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+4)^{2}=-3(x-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,-4).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+4=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-3(x-3) \\\\ 4p(x-3) & =-3(x-3) \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+4=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-\\frac{3}{2}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30ebd37e-c666-5413-8dba-6706d27b3ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486695} {:answer/id #uuid "5471d6a5-d5f1-5e64-b0d4-d247884f4266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5={t}^{2},\\,y+2=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486776} {:answer/id #uuid "68408a08-0e06-57f9-927a-8ec79117001a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486821} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4a770e-5b76-5688-9d7d-d367b29628f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486837} {:answer/id #uuid "956916df-320e-5b5e-b828-825e40cf6ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{8}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e4a770e-5b76-5688-9d7d-d367b29628f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486837} :answer-field/id #uuid "d889d998-6d7a-58b2-a490-fd41b8228c67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da2c9d27-2db1-52e6-a2e3-b28e1798b755" :question/math-academy-id "q-84846" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(x+5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-5,-2).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx+5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+2=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+5) \\\\ 4p(x+5) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+5) \\\\ 4p & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+5=(\\frac{1}{8}){t}^{2},\\,y+2=2(\\frac{1}{8})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+5=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99cd9563-54d3-5b7d-8b1c-d33708f1a679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=3(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486942} {:answer/id #uuid "b168e266-4a99-588e-9178-cf1013be9d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486989} {:answer/id #uuid "c80b48be-9145-50c4-a5a3-de75c6e5b2a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487039} {:answer/id #uuid "dab38c33-dab6-5256-8c24-9c5a85a4e93b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487070} {:answer/id #uuid "ed78aad1-fdf7-515b-89c3-467256a0cee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=3(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99cd9563-54d3-5b7d-8b1c-d33708f1a679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=3(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486942} :answer-field/id #uuid "8297d34d-1705-5fb7-8698-40fca163d6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c50b5890-601e-5b61-a526-2b2221f83863" :question/math-academy-id "q-84852" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{4(x+1)}{3},\\,t=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2(y+2)}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(y+2)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{4(x+1)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{4(x+1)}{3} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot (\\frac{4(x+1)}{3}) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =3(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
